Setting. X is a projective line over a field k of characteristic different from 2. The parenthetical says it suffices that k be closed under square roots, presumably as a weakening of 'algebraically closed'. G = Aut_k(X) = PGL_2(k), V is a Klein four-subgroup (Grothendieck writes D_2), and the standard model is V = {z, -z, 1/z, -1/z}. Each nontrivial σ in V has a 2-point fixed set X^σ: {0,∞}, {±1} and {±i} respectively. The page continues the previous one, where Γ = Nor_G(V) sits in an extension 1→V→Γ→Aut(V)≅S_3→1. Splittings of that extension correspond to the four pairs of antipodal faces of the octahedron Oc(V) with vertex set S(V) = ∪_{σ∈V*} X^σ.

(1) Γ(V) acts on Oc(V), hence on the 4-element set I(V) of antipodal face pairs. The map Γ(V) → S_{I(V)} is an isomorphism; this is the classical identification of the rotation group of the octahedron/cube with S_4 via the four body diagonals. Restricted to V it gives a simply transitive action, so I(V) is a V-torsor. Since |I| = 4, the 'affine' permutation group of this V-torsor, AGL_2(F_2), is all of S_4. That matches Grothendieck's notation S^V_{I(V)}. He justifies this by the combinatorial theory of the octahedron, or by direct check in the standard case.

(2) Proposition. There are mutually inverse bijections between:
- (a) Klein four-subgroups V of G;
- (b) subgroups Γ of G isomorphic to S_4;
- (c) 6-point subsets S of X(k) that are 'octahedral', i.e. such that (X,S) ≅ (P^1_k, {0,∞,±1,±i}).

The maps are:
- Γ = Nor_G(V);
- V = the unique normal Klein subgroup of Γ;
- S = the ramification (fixed-point) set of the action of V on X.

NB: Γ has a larger ramification set. Besides S (6 points) it contains the 'edge midpoints' (12 points) and 'face centres' (8 points); these are the classical orbits of sizes 6, 12, 8 of the octahedral group.

(3) Structure on S. S carries an antipodism a_S = a_V, characterized by: for each σ in V*, a_S swaps the two points of X^σ. The faces are the 3-subsets S' with S' ∪ a(S') = S, and this defines an octahedral structure on S. Γ is its group of orientation-preserving automorphisms. The previous page shows that choosing an orientation amounts to choosing a square root of -1.

(4) Margin. The category of pairs (X,V) (equivalently (X,Γ) or (X,S)) is equivalent to the category of (oriented) octahedral combinatorial structures, with orientation matched to the choice of i in {±i}. This is a rigidity statement: the combinatorial octahedron determines (P^1, S) up to unique isomorphism. It is a small instance of the dessins/combinatorial-to-algebraic dictionary that underlies the 'chirurgie des surfaces conformes' project of this dossier. The next page goes on to describe octahedral structures purely in terms of S with a V-action, and to distinguish V-orbits I in X∖S from the torsor I(V).
